+### Task 3: failures, poisoning and store errors
+
+Spec §4.7, §6.1 step 2 and the paragraph after the steps, §6.3's stderr line, and §7. A `FileError`
+fails only its own file. After `POISON_AFTER` failures the file is poisoned and later runs skip it
+without opening it. Any other exception rolls back the file in hand and propagates.
+
+**Files:**
+- Modify: `readings/runner.py`
+- Test: `tests/test_runner.py`
+
+**Interfaces:**
+- Consumes: everything Task 2 produced. `parse.FileError` and its messages from Task 1.
+- Produces: `runner.POISON_AFTER = 3`. `run` keeps its signature and now also writes the stderr
+  line `"<name>: <state> (<failures>): <detail>\n"` to `sys.stderr`, looked up at call time so
+  `contextlib.redirect_stderr` captures it. Marker detail for a failed file is `str(FileError)`.
